Fallout from k101, recorded so the orchestrator's stated reason for capturing response headers is not silently unmet.

The k101 decision was: capture the response HEADERS alongside the bodies so t/12_rate_limit.t
can stop hand-writing them. The captures are landed (t/data/*.headers.json, five files), but
they cannot serve that purpose:

AKI.IO sends NO rate-limit headers on any of its three wires. The captured header sets are
exactly access-control-allow-origin, connection, content-length, content-type, date, server.
No x-ratelimit-* (Role::OpenAICompatible.pm:569-594 reads six of those) and no
anthropic-ratelimit-* (Role::AnthropicCompatible.pm:582-612 reads twelve).

What t/28_aki_fixtures.t does assert with them -- the most they can prove -- is that
has_rate_limit stays false after a real 200 driven through the shims' own
_parse_rate_limit_headers. A future AKI capture that DOES carry the headers will fail there
rather than pass unnoticed.

t/12_rate_limit.t is therefore unchanged and still builds every header by hand, which is the
same class of gap k101 is about: the parser is only ever fed payloads written to fit it.

To close this, a capture is needed from a provider that actually emits the headers -- OpenAI,
Anthropic or Groq all do. That means a live call with a key Langertha's agents do not have
standing approval for (memory rule: only AKI.IO is pre-approved), so this is blocked on the
maintainer choosing a provider and authorising one cheap request. One request per wire family
(one OpenAI-shaped, one Anthropic-shaped) is enough; the bodies are irrelevant, only the
headers matter.

